We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min \left\{\int_{\Omega}f\left(x, Dv(x)\right)dx\,:\, v\in
  \mathcal{K}_{\zeta}\cap W^{1,p}(\Omega, \mathfrak{g}(x))\right\},
  \end{equation*}
  under the assumption that the function $f$ is convex and satisfies a specific growth condition with respect to the gradient variable, and that the boundary data $\zeta$ and the data on the obstacle $\psi$ have controlled difference differentiability. If the target domain is of a higher dimension, we have achieved a step down in the regularity of space.
